  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Perfect-Cube Adjustments and Integrated Root Problems",
    "description": "Find factors that complete a cube and combine root methods in multi-step problems.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Use prime-exponent groups of three to find the least multiplier or divisor that makes a positive integer a perfect cube, and check divisibility wording precisely. Then integrate at least two approved chapter skills in fresh problems: square-root or cube-root factorization with decimal scaling, nearest-square reasoning followed by an expression calculation, radical laws with conjugate simplification, or a root equation with a domain check. Do not turn this into a question bank or introduce unrelated exponent theory. Learners should plan the order of operations, show why each factor or identity is valid, retain exact values until needed, and check the result by squaring/cubing or substitution."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Perfect-cube adjustments use prime exponents in groups of three. Integrated problems ask you to combine that structure with square roots, decimal place value, and exact radical methods.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a perfect cube, each prime exponent is divisible by 3. Factor 1,080=2³×3³×5; multiplying by 5² completes the group, so 1,080×25=27,000=30³. The smallest factor completes each incomplete exponent group.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
